          %h2#idea Idea
          %p A lattice-ordered group is a group which is also a lattice in a compatible way.
          %h2#partially_ordered_groups Partially ordered groups.
          %p
            First we introduce
            = succeed "." do
              %em partially ordered groups
          %p
            A group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is said to be
            %em partially ordered
            if it is equipped with a partial order,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\leq h
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi k
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi h
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\cdot k\leq h\cdot k
            and also
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\cdot g\leq k\cdot h
            for any
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g,h,k \in G
          %h2#positive_elements Positive elements
          %p
            The set,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mo +
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^+=\{g\in G\mid g\geq 1\}
            is called the
            %em positive cone
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G,\leq)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\leq h
            if and only if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi hg
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mo *
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hg^{-1}\in G^*\cup \{0\}
          %h2#latticeordered_groups Lattice-ordered groups.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G,\leq)
            is a partially ordered group, and the partial order is a lattice, then we say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em lattice-ordered group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b
            and a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meet"} meet
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\wedge b
          %p
            Many sources abbreviate ‘lattice-ordered group’ to ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            \-group’.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The group operation distibutes over both
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∧
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            %li
              %p
                Inversion reverses the order so it
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi h
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\le h
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi h
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ≤
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^{-1}\leq g^{-1}
                = succeed "s.)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+groupoid"} ordered groupoid
            %li
              %p
                A sort of de Morgan’s law holds, so
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi b
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi a
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ∧
                        %msup
                          %mi b
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a\vee b)^{-1}= a^{-1}\wedge b^{-1}
            %li
              %p The compatibility of the group multiplication with the lattice structure interpretas as saying that left and right multiplication by elements of the group give automorphisms of the lattice structure.
          %h2#latticeordered_groups_and_residuated_lattices Lattice-ordered groups and residuated lattices
          %p
            We note that given any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x / y = x\cdot y^{-1}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo \
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\backslash x = y^{-1}\cdot x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\cdot y \leq z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                  %mo \
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\leq x\backslash z
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\leq z / y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^{-1}= 1 / x
            \- which is neat!
          %p
            This gives that any lattice-ordered group gives a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/residuated+lattice"} residuated lattice
          %p
            The lattice-ordered groups form a variet ub the category of residuated lattices that is axiomatised by the identity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\cdot (1/x) = x
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+group"} ordered group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice-ordered+abelian+group"} lattice-ordered abelian group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice-ordered+ring"} lattice-ordered ring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleene+algebra"} Kleene algebra
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article that considered ordered groups in the non-commutative case is by Garrett Birkhoff:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Garrett+Birkhoff"}> Garrett Birkhoff
              \.
              %em Lattice-ordered groups,
              Annals of Math. 43, (1942) pp. 298 - 331.
          %p He later considered Lattice-ordered Lie groups.
          %p There are several monographs or books on the subject of lattice ordered groups.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Marlow Anderson and Todd Feil,
                %em Lattice Ordered Groups: An Introduction,
                Reidel Texts in the Mathematical Sciences, vol 4, (1988), (doi:10.1007/978-94-009-2871-8).
            %li
              %p
                V. M. Kopytov, N. Ya. Medvedev,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Theory of Lattice-Ordered Groups
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-015-8304-6"} doi
